daemon: Haptic intensity and click force settings for touchpads

Windows sends two feature reports to a haptic touchpad when it enables the
device (and again when the user changes the setting): the haptic feedback
intensity and the click force. Linux never does, so the pad stays at whatever
the firmware picked.

Adds [Touchpad] HapticIntensity (0 to 100, the Windows FeedbackIntensity, whose
default is 50) and [Touchpad] ClickForce (low, medium or high, the Windows
ClickForceSensitivity 0 / 50 / 100, default medium). Both are unset by default,
and an unset key sends nothing, so the firmware setting stays in place.

The reports are found in the HID descriptor by usage, not by report ID: the
click force is the Digitizer page Button Press Threshold (0x0D / 0xB0) feature
report, the intensity the Haptics page Intensity (0x0E / 0x23) feature report.
The bit position of the field follows from the fields in front of it and the
value is scaled onto the logical range of the field (click force: low = minimum,
medium = middle, high = maximum). A device without the report is logged once
and otherwise ignored.

Touchpads only. The settings are sent after the runner enables multitouch at
startup and after every mode watchdog recovery (0004), the same moments Windows
re-sends them. Every apply is logged at info level.

